<section id="faq" style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="kick">FAQ</p>
    <h2>Questions devs <em>actually</em> ask.</h2>
    <div class="faq" data-reveal>
      <details><summary>Does it work on a Claude Max plan?</summary><p>Yes. On a flat plan you don’t save money, you save quota: grunt work stops eating your Opus limits, so the plan lasts longer.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Will it slow me down?</summary><p>Routing decisions run locally in tens of milliseconds. If Horsy fails or is slow, everything runs as before. It never blocks you.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if it routes something wrong?</summary><p>The check catches it and the task steps up one rung, with the reason. If a task type keeps losing, Horsy turns itself off for that type. You can also pin a model per task, or run <code>/horsy off</code>.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does my code leave my machine?</summary><p>Not to Horsy’s servers. Routing data is metadata only. If you use a remote judge for tasks without tests, it sees only the snippet it checks.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it beat just using Sonnet for everything?</summary><p>We don’t know yet, and we won’t claim it until we’ve tested it on real coding sessions. The receipts will show it either way, including when the number is negative.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it touch my main session?</summary><p>No. Opus keeps planning in your main session. Horsy only routes the subagents it delegates to.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
